<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #1a5276;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our New Classmates – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 5px;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our New Classmates</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> What was Your Previous School Like?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Explain a useful skill. Interactive: Talk about preferences and familiar topics in a guided class discussion.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can explain a useful skill learned at school or in the community (e.g., "I learned how to play the drum in music class." "Liam taught me how to solve math problems faster.").
Can talk about preferences when discussing school activities and routines (e.g., "I prefer morning classes because I have more energy." "I like math more than science because I enjoy solving problems.").
Can participate in guided discussions to compare school experiences with classmates (e.g., "In my previous school, we had longer breaks." "My new school has a bigger library than my old one.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and asks them to stand up. Students walk around the classroom, and when the music stops, they pair up with the closest classmate. In pairs, students take turns sharing one thing they *used to do* or *learned* at their *previous school* or *community*. (e.g., "I *used to play* soccer every recess." "I *learned* how to bake cookies with my grandma.") Students repeat with a new partner twice. This activates vocabulary like 'school', 'community', 'recess', 'play', 'learn', 'used to'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to express preferences and comparisons. "In my *old school*, we *had* a *small library*. This school *has* a *bigger* one. I *prefer* the new school's *library*. What about you?" The teacher uses gestures and clear pronunciation, emphasizing the endings of plurals and third person singular verbs with /s/, /z/, and /ɪz/ as examples when speaking (e.g., "classes" /ɪz/, "books" /s/, "friends" /z/).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'My new school *is bigger than* my old one,' am I saying both schools are the same size?" (Expected answer: No.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'prefer' mean? Does it mean you like something *more* or *less*?" (Expected answer: More.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our New Classmates".</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher presents a short dialogue on the whiteboard or through audio about two *new classmates* introducing themselves and discussing their *previous schools*. The dialogue uses present and past simple, comparatives, and key vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple dialogue for an interview, displayed as a text on the board or a handout.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example Dialogue Excerpt:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><em>Ana: "Hi, I'm Ana. I *moved from* Chiriquí. What was your *previous school* like, Juan?"</em></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><em>Juan: "Hello Ana! I'm Juan. My *old school* *was* in Santiago. It *was small*, but the *teachers were* very *friendly*. We *used to have* music *classes* every Friday. What about yours?"</em></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><em>Ana: "My *previous school* *was bigger*, and the *playground was more colorful*. I *preferred* art *class*. I *learned* how to paint really well. I *think* this new school *is* probably *the most modern* school I've ever seen!"</em></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Participating in guided discussions about preferences and routines; Comparing schools, classmates, or communities using comparatives and superlatives.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher asks simple gist questions after reading/listening to the dialogue:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Where was Juan's *old school*?" (Santiago)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What *subject* did Ana *prefer*?" (Art)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was Ana's *previous school* *bigger* or *smaller* than Juan's?" (Bigger)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Accuracy focus on Speaking.</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using Present and Past Simple for habits and descriptions, Comparatives and superlatives, Future tense (affirmative and interrogative sentences), and the target vocabulary to respond to questions and express preferences about schools and activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ol>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion:</b> The teacher provides sentence stems on the board related to school experiences and preferences. Students complete them orally in pairs, ensuring correct grammar and vocabulary.
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"My *previous school* *was* _______, but my new one *is* ________." (e.g., small, bigger)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I *used to* _______ every day, but now I _______." (e.g., walk to school, take the bus)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I *prefer* _______ *subject* because _______." (e.g., Math, I enjoy solving problems)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"In my *community*, I *learned* how to _______." (e.g., play basketball, paint)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Next month, I *will join* the _______ activity."</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Q&A Chain Drill:</b> Students sit in a circle. The teacher starts by asking one student, "What *was* your *previous school like*?" The student answers, then turns to the next student and asks a question using a comparative or superlative (e.g., "Is your new *classroom* *cleaner than* your old one?"). The chain continues, encouraging varied use of target grammar and vocabulary. The teacher monitors for correct pronunciation of /s/, /z/, /ɪz/ endings.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - "Ask and Compare":</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a small card with questions like:
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Where *did you come from*?"</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What *was* your *previous school like*?" (e.g., "It *was noisy* and *small*.")</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What *subject* *did you use to* *study* that you *don't study* now?"</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What *activity* *did you prefer* at *recess*?"</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What *useful skill* *did you learn* in your *community*?"</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What *will you do* this *weekend*?"</li>
                            </ul>
                            Students take turns asking and answering, then briefly summarize their partner's responses to each other (e.g., "Juan's *previous school was* *smaller* than mine, but *he prefers* the new *cafeteria*.") This activity ensures practice in responding to questions and comparing experiences.
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-20 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our New Classmates".</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Meet a Classmate – Interview and Share"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will work in groups of three or four. Each student interviews two other *classmates* in their group about their *previous school* experiences, preferred *subjects* or *activities*, and *useful skills learned*. They use the target grammar (Present/Past Simple, Comparatives/Superlatives, Future Tense) and vocabulary to ask and answer questions and take brief notes. After the interviews, each student presents one interesting fact about each of their interviewed *classmates'* *previous school* or a *skill* they *learned* to the rest of their group. (e.g., "Maria *used to go* to a *quieter school*. She *learned* how to *play* the guitar. Her *old playground was smaller* than ours. She *will join* the music club next month.") This activity explicitly focuses on interactive speaking, presenting personal and classmate information, and comparing school experiences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme: "What was Your Previous School Like?" and Scenario: "Our New Classmates".</b> The task directly addresses the theme by having students discuss and compare their past school experiences and preferences with their new classmates. It fosters connection and understanding within the new classroom environment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency, collaborative interaction, responding to questions, comparing information, and communicative task completion.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Meet a Classmate" activity, focusing on participation and use of target language. Students will also complete a peer assessment based on a simple rubric.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in the group interview and sharing activity, demonstrating their ability to ask and answer questions about previous school experiences and preferences, and to explain a useful skill learned. They will also present summarized information about their classmates.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation & Peer Assessment):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (especially /s/, /z/, /ɪz/ endings).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., 'previous school', 'subject', 'prefer', 'used to', 'learned', 'community', comparatives like 'bigger', 'smaller', superlatives like 'most interesting').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in explaining a useful skill and comparing experiences.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and active participation in group discussions.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper before leaving:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What *was* the *most interesting* thing you *learned* about one of your *classmates'* *previous schools* today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What *useful skill* *did you explain* to your *classmates*?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy or difficult for you when talking about your *previous school* or a *skill* you *learned*?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How *will* you *practice* speaking about your experiences with *new classmates* in the future?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> Most students actively participated and used the target vocabulary and grammar to compare their previous school experiences. Some students still struggled with consistent use of 'used to' and forming comparatives/superlatives correctly in spontaneous speech. Pronunciation of /s/, /z/, /ɪz/ endings improved with modeling, but requires continued reinforcement. I need to provide more explicit sentence frames for comparisons in the next speaking activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In Lesson 4 (Writing Composition), students will use the information gathered and practiced in this speaking lesson to write a short paragraph comparing their current school with their *previous school* or describing a *classmate's previous school* and *skills learned*, consolidating the vocabulary and grammar learned.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>